# FlintTrade — desktop

> Electron 40 shell with verified local-source bootstrap and guardian lifecycle
> Serves: selected loopback origin only | Branch: follow the current task context

## Quick commands

```bash
make desktop-test       # strict Electron TypeScript + full Vitest
make desktop-build      # verify resources and bundle main/preload
make desktop-package    # package and verify this host's installer
make desktop-dev        # run against the managed source bootstrap
```

## Architecture

- `electron/main.ts` wires the singleton, bootstrap, guardian supervision,
  windows, tray, hotkey, notifications and both update flows.
- `electron/preload.ts` exposes named `window.flintDesktop` methods only.
- `resources/bootstrap/` contains checksum-bound platform bootstrap entrypoints.
- First launch builds the active source at `~/.flinttrade/src/FlintTrade`; the
  user workspace stays separate and never participates in promotion or
  ordinary uninstall.
- Source/runtime updates stage and health-prove a sibling candidate before
  promotion. Electron-shell updates require a complete release and checksum,
  then hand off to the identity-bound installer.
- Close hides to tray; explicit quit drains the guardian. Native notifications
  consume strictly parsed `FLINTTRADE_NOTIFY\t<title>\t<body>` lines.

## House rules that bite

- Electron owns machine lifecycle only. Trading, broker and safety authority
  stays in the Python backend.
- Every IPC handler validates its sender; never expose Node, raw `ipcRenderer`
  or a generic command string to the renderer.
- Never mutate the running source checkout or include user data in
  promotion/rollback.
- No complete Electron release is published yet. Do not present beta.13's
  retired assets as source-bootstrap installers.
- A macOS package is not Windows/Linux proof. Cross-platform evidence belongs
  to deterministic tests and CI/contributor runs.

